      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the least whole number of hours \\( n \\) greater than 4 hours such that \\( n \\) is \"clock equivalent\" to its square \\( n^2 \\). This means that the time \\( n \\) hours is the same as the time \\( n^2 \\) hours on a 12-hour clock.\n\nWe start by expressing this condition mathematically:\n\\[ n \\equiv n^2 \\pmod{12} \\]\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\\[ n^2 - n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\n\\[ n(n-1) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\]\nThis means that \\( n(n-1) \\) must be divisible by 12. Since \\( n \\) and \\( n-1 \\) are consecutive integers, one of them must be divisible by 3 and one of them must be divisible by 4. Additionally, since the product of two consecutive integers is always even, \\( n \\) or \\( n-1 \\) must also be divisible by 2, so \\( n \\) or \\( n-1 \\) must be divisible by 2 again. Therefore, \\( n \\) or \\( n-1 \\) must be divisible by 6 (since 6 is the least common multiple of 3 and 4).\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n \\) greater than 4 that satisfies this condition. We can check the values of \\( n \\) one by one:\n\nFor \\( n = 5 \\):\n\\[ 5 \\times 4 = 20 \\]\nSince \\( 20 \\div 12 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\), \\( 5 \\times 4 \\not\\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\nFor \\( n = 6 \\):\n\\[ 6 \\times 5 = 30 \\]\nSince \\( 30 \\div 12 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 6 \\), \\( 6 \\times 5 \\not\\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\nFor \\( n = 7 \\):\n\\[ 7 \\times 6 = 42 \\]\nSince \\( 42 \\div 12 = 3 \\text{ remainder } 6 \\), \\( 7 \\times 6 \\not\\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\nFor \\( n = 8 \\):\n\\[ 8 \\times 7 = 56 \\]\nSince \\( 56 \\div 12 = 4 \\text{ remainder } 8 \\), \\( 8 \\times 7 \\not\\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\nFor \\( n = 9 \\):\n\\[ 9 \\times 8 = 72 \\]\nSince \\( 72 \\div 12 = 6 \\text{ remainder } 0 \\), \\( 9 \\times 8 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{12} \\).\n\nTherefore, the smallest whole number of hours that is greater than 4 hours and is \"clock equivalent\" to its square is \\( \\boxed{9} \\).",
